<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jasna Góra Monastery</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1382">1382</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jasna Góra is a Pauline monastery on a hill in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czestochowa">Częstochowa</a>, southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, founded in 1382 by Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wladyslaw-ii-of-opole">Władysław II of Opole</a>, who brought monks from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>. It houses the icon of the Black Madonna. Its successful defence against the Swedes in 1655, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-deluge">Deluge</a>, made it a national symbol, and it remains Poland's most important place of pilgrimage.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jasna-gora-monastery.jpg" alt="Jasna Góra Monastery" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jerzy Szota · CC BY-SA 3.0 pl · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cz%C4%99stochowa_klasztor_Jasna_G%C3%B3ra-2162.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
